Spring Boot 1.x (2014): Revolutionized Java development by introducing the concepts of auto-configuration and fat executable JARs with embedded Tomcat containers. It allowed developers to ditch heavy XML setups and bootstrap web applications using predefined starter dependencies.
Spring Boot 2.x (2018): Rewrote the runtime landscape by natively integrating Spring Framework 5, Java 8 baselines, and WebFlux for reactive architectures. It also completely overhauled Actuator metrics by integrating Micrometer and introducing major WebJar optimizations.
Spring Boot 3.x (2022): Set a modern standard by dropping Java 8/11 and enforcing a strict Java 17 baseline alongside the jakarta.* API namespace. It debuted native compilation via GraalVM and introduced first-class support for virtual threads from Project Loom.
Spring Boot 4.0 (2025): Transitioned entirely to Spring Framework 7.0 and Jakarta EE 11 baselines while remaining backwards-compatible with Java 17+. It brought deep structural performance enhancements to OpenTelemetry observability, containerised distributions, and native workflows.
Spring Boot 4.1 (2026): The current production-ready stable standard that features built-in gRPC auto-configuration and automated HTTP-client SSRF mitigation. It adds async context propagation for @Async routines, lazy datasource connections, and supports up to Java 26.